Yeah. That’s a real social dynamic: pluralistic ignorance.

A group can privately share the same concern, preference, joke, doubt, or interpretation while each person assumes everyone else feels differently. So nobody wants to be the first mover.

Then one person says it and suddenly:

\text{private agreement}
\rightarrow
\text{public signal}
\rightarrow
\text{rapid coordination}

That can feel like “everyone changed their mind,” when really the hidden state was already there.

The useful caution is that silence is ambiguous. It can mean agreement, fear, uncertainty, indifference, or actual disagreement. So the cleanest move is often to create a low-cost way to reveal the latent state without forcing conformity—anonymous polling, independent answers first, or simply asking, “Who else was thinking something similar?”

That way you uncover the shared pattern without manufacturing it.